,-3.576272320060525e-06,-0.1404305100440979,-0.02227547951042652,-5.602820692729438e-06,-0.005904375575482845,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-0.027294013649225235,0.0,-1.8596476365928538e-05,0.0,0.0,0.0,-5.4596363042946905e-05,0.0,-9.536738616588991e-07,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-1.7881377516459906e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,4340,1657,13530,315,6785,25780,4930,64,8402,15087,26553,57960,37018,90,16,15170,64,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,65,92,34433,37018,90,17,15170,16,22,31716,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many pairs of positive integers $(a,b)$ satisfy $\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b}=\\frac{2}{17}$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"bf0fb41e464845b02393af890edca100aaa7daa7696565f4db6eeecc9abf3b0c"},"seconds":19.232465744018555,"seed":1109205016354403373,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{2}{17}\\) for positive integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we start by finding a common denominator for the left side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b}{ab} = \\frac{2}{17}\n\\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\n\\[\n17(a + b) = 2ab\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2ab - 17a - 17b = 0\n\\]\n\nTo solve this, we add 289 to both sides to facilitate factoring:\n\n\\[\n2ab - 17a - 17b + 289 = 289\n\\]\n\nThis can be factored as:\n\n\\[\n(2a - 17)(b - \\frac{17}{2}) = \\frac{289}{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 2 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2a - 17)(2b - 17) = 289\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find all pairs of factors of 289. Since \\(289 = 17^2\\), the factor pairs of 289 are \\((1, 289)\\), \\((17, 17)\\), and \\((289, 1)\\). We will solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) using each pair.\n\n1. For the pair \\((1, 289)\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 17 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 17 = 289\n\\]\n\nSolving these, we get:\n\n\\[\n2a = 18 \\implies a = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n2b = 306 \\implies b = 153\n\\]\n\nSo, one solution is \\((a, b) = (9, 153)\\).\n\n2. For the pair \\((289, 1)\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 17 = 289 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 17 = 1\n\\]\n\nSolving these, we get:\n\n\\[\n2a = 306 \\implies a = 153\n\\]\n\\[\n2b = 18 \\implies b = 9\n\\]\n\nSo, another solution is \\((a, b) = (153, 9)\\).\n\n3. For the pair \\((17, 17)\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 17 = 17 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 17 = 17\n\\]\n\nSolving these, we get:\n\n\\[\n2a = 34 \\implies a = 17\n\\]\n\\[\n2b = 34 \\implies b = 17\n\\]\n\nSo, the third solution is \\((a, b) = (17, 17)\\).\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy the equation are \\((9, 153)\\), \\((153, 9)\\), and \\((17, 17)\\). Therefore, there are 3 pairs of positive integers \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy the equation.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:370"}
